Another thing: BillR (the guy is a genius) casually mentioned something that
answers the question I had in my mind for a while viz.,
" How is it that processors are cache coherent / use MOESI and yet can
  only provide Causal Consistency and not Total Store Order"

Simple, threaded processors like Intel share the post commit buffer. Stores here
haven't paid the coherence cost (havent committed). So if another thread on the same
core reads from it he gets to see stores other cores havent aka Causal Consistency.
Of course if the read flushed it to L1 then MESI would kick in and ensure all cores
got the results. But no, this optimization I guess helps them which is why 
Rick Hudson (Intel) went around JMM archives polling if Causal Consistency broke anybody.



